<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Morality of Human Acts" cic="CCC 1749-1761" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">To use after class or in group discussion.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What are the three sources of morality for every human act?</li>
<li>What makes an act &laquo;intrinsically evil&raquo;?</li>
<li>Can a good intention make an evil act good? Why or why not?</li>
<li>What is the difference between invincible and vincible ignorance?</li>
<li>What are the four conditions for the principle of double effect?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. Think of a decision you made recently. What was the object, your intention, and the circumstances? Were all three aligned with the good?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Have you ever tried to justify a wrong action with a &laquo;good intention&raquo;? Looking back, how do you see it now?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. What areas of your moral life might be affected by vincible ignorance? What could you do to learn more?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. How does knowing that &laquo;the end does not justify the means&raquo; change how you approach difficult decisions?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. What is one area of your life where you could act with greater integrity &mdash; aligning your object, intention, and circumstances with what is good?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. Draw a &laquo;Tree of Actions&raquo;</h4>
<p>Draw a tree with three main branches labeled Object, Intention, and Circumstances. On each branch, write examples from your own life. Add leaves that are the &laquo;fruits&raquo; (consequences). Reflect on whether your tree bears good fruit.</p>

<h4>B. Write a Letter to Your Future Self</h4>
<p>Write a letter to yourself one year from now. Describe the kind of person you want to be &mdash; someone whose actions, intentions, and circumstances align with the Gospel. Seal it and open it in one year.</p>

<h4>C. Weekly Examination of Conscience</h4>
<p>This week, each night ask yourself three questions: (1) What did I do today? (2) Why did I do it? (3) What were the circumstances? End with an act of contrition and a resolution for tomorrow.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Memory Verse</h3>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p>&laquo;Either make the tree good and its fruit good, or make the tree bad and its fruit bad; for the tree is known by its fruit&raquo;</p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">Matthew 12:33</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Prayer for This Week</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>
Lord Jesus Christ,<br />
You who always did what was pleasing to the Father,<br />
teach me to act with integrity.<br />
Purify my intentions, guide my choices,<br />
and help me to see the difference between good and evil.<br />
May my actions bear good fruit<br />
for Your glory and the salvation of souls.<br />
Amen.
</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

</LessonLayout>
